# XSH pthread_key_create()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Keys are used only between their creation and deletion (anything else
# is undefined). Where several destructors run, their order is
# unspecified and is not checked.

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_create.001
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_key_create() returns 0 and stores a key whose value is
           NULL in every live thread: in the creating thread and in a
           thread that already existed, also when a key that thread had
           set was deleted just before.
test       t/001-null-in-all.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_create.002
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread created after a key was made, by a thread that set a
           non-NULL value for it, starts with NULL for that key.
test       t/002-null-in-new-thread.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_create.003
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When a thread ends, the destructor of a key it set to a non-NULL
           value is called once with that value; the destructor of a key
           whose value in that thread is NULL (never set, or set back to
           NULL) is not called.
test       t/003-destructor.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_create.004
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/limits.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A destructor that sets its key to a non-NULL value again on its
           first three calls is called again each time: four calls in all,
           and no fifth once the value stays NULL.
note       Four is {_POSIX_THREAD_DESTRUCTOR_ITERATIONS}, the least
           {PTHREAD_DESTRUCTOR_ITERATIONS} may be, so no conforming
           system may stop before the fourth call.
test       t/004-iterations.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_create.005
anchor     functions/pthread_key_create.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       With {PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX} known (sysconf(), else <limits.h>),
           creating that many plus one keys fails at least once, and the
           first failure is EAGAIN or ENOMEM.
note       Keys the implementation uses itself may make the failure come
           sooner; both errors are listed as shall-fail and may apply at
           once (XSH 2.3). NOTINUSE when the limit is unknown or above
           65536.
test       t/005-limit.c
